{"chunk_id": "0bacb04614d8", "text": "This section depicts the Pandavas overwhelming the forces of Jayadratha after the abduction of Draupadi. As the battle erupts, the five brothers fight with terrifying efficiency: Bhima smashes enemies and elephants with his mace, Arjuna cuts down hundreds with relentless archery, Nakula and Sahadeva slaughter warriors in close combat, and Yudhishthira himself kills a charging foe despite losing his chariot horses. The battlefield becomes a scene of carnage as armies collapse before them. Terrified, Jayadratha abandons Draupadi and flees into the forest. Draupadi is recovered and placed safely on a chariot, while Bhima begins hunting the fleeing king. Arjuna urges him to focus on Jayadratha rather than the retreating troops. Yet Yudhishthira reminds Bhima not to kill Jayadratha, invoking family ties through Dussala and Gandhari, introducing a tension between vengeance and restraint.\nThe Pandavas devastate Jayadratha\u2019s forces, rescue Draupadi, and pursue the fleeing Sindhu king, though Yudhishthira urges Bhima to spare him for family ties.\nThemes: battle heroism, rescue of Draupadi, vengeance and restraint, family ties in war", "vector": [-0.009205823764204979, -0.021339572966098785, -0.008091364987194538, 0.008051798678934574, -0.015945330262184143, 0.031125707551836967, -0.008605730719864368, -0.0008853021427057683, -0.008368331007659435, -0.029332023113965988, 0.0060372003354132175, 0.010425793007016182, 0.013663657940924168, 0.012437094002962112, 0.00706922821700573, 0.018068736419081688, 0.034713078290224075, -0.008084770292043686, 0.020877962931990623, -0.022618893533945084, -0.0030153028201311827, -0.013076753355562687, -0.0010295553365722299, 0.03133672848343849, -0.02856706827878952, 0.035847317427396774, 0.005470079369843006, -0.014850655570626259, 0.01830613613128662, 0.0069637177512049675, -0.01901833526790142, -0.005446998868137598, 0.00892226304858923, -0.00542391836643219, -0.013518580235540867, 0.00096690823